[Music]  about women's reproductive rights and  religious liberty I'm joined now by  three of people who know a lot about  this attorney Gloria Allred attorney  Kristen Wagner of the Alliance defending  freedom and professor Steve Vermeil a  law professor at American University and  let me start with you if I could Gloria  Allred what did you make of this  morning's arguments well first of all of  course we we know that we can never tell  how a justice is going to rule based on  the questions that they ask because  sometimes they're asking questions which  are intended to have the answers  influence the vote of another justice  but having said that of course I do  agree with justice Ginsburg's questions  and justice Sotomayor questions which  essentially incorporates Justice  Ginsburg question that didn't the cotton  Congress through the Affordable Care Act  intend to have women be able to be  afforded contraceptive coverage seamless  health coverage and not require them to  have to go out and search for coverage  outside of the health plan that would be  provided by their employer and and I  think their answer would be yes that's  what the intention of Congress was to  make sure that women's contraceptive  coverage would be covered and Kristen  Wagner let me ask you what did you see  out of this as the justices sought to  find a balance of religious liberty and  women's reproductive rights Kristen well  I think what the justices are trying to  do is assume that Congress meant what it  said and what it didn't say in the  statute is that contraceptives must be  covered by everyone and I think the  unique aspect of this particular  argument is that those who argued in  favor of essentially requiring nuns to  dole out birth  they were happy with the Obama  administration's exemptions early on  when they provided exemptions for  churches and others they said oh yes the  administration has the authority to  determine who gets exemptions but now  that the administration has changed they  don't like how the agency is  interpreting those exemptions so I want  to bring this back this isn't about  access to birth control it's about  whether you a statute gives the  authority to an agency to decide these  types of issues and we know they haven't  produced even one woman who hasn't been  able to get coverage that's right that  was very I thought very striking that  they argued that Professor Steve Vermeil  you covered the Supreme Court for years  for The Wall Street Journal tell me what  you saw between the lines here well it  was obviously very lively sometimes  technical argument I think it's hard to  tell from listening to it whether the  court is going to decide this on a  administrative basis did the the Trump  administration and expanding the rule  follow the proper procedures or whether  the court will be more influenced by the  the competing images that we heard of  the effect of the rule that on the one  hand the liberal justices depicting  thousands of women who may not be be  able to get access to contraception and  on the other hand more conservative  justices depicting it as a question of  the religious freedom of employers not  to be forced to provide contraception I  think it's hard to see which way the  court will go and Gloria Allred let me  ask you about Ruth Bader Ginsburg she  was phoning in from her hospital bed  where she was seeking treatment for a  gall bladder issue but seemed to be very  lively and very much in the middle of  this debate yes she did and I have had  the honor and the pleasure of meeting  Justice Ginsburg and having dinner with  her at an event and actually just seeing  her and speaking very briefly to her in  February of this year at an event  we're an award was being made a woman of  leadership award in her honor she is  persistent she is a fighter she is going  to phone in from a hospital bed or  wherever she is because she takes her  duty as a Justice of the Supreme Court  very very seriously and you know she is  my Shiro and the Shiro the hero of so  many millions of women not only in this  United States but also in the world so  I'm proud of her  I I will say that if there is any  discretion of any agency let's assume  there is only for the purpose of  discussion I think the Justice is made  clear that it can't be capricious it  can't be arbitrary it has to be  reasonable so I the argument here is  that what the Trump administration has  expanded in terms of exemptions is not  reasonable and is arbitrary and is  capricious so I do think the you know  even if no woman has said I can't get  birth control that's not the way I think  to decide this because it's clear that  if their health plans do not allow them  to get coverage for contraceptives that  many women will be deprived of their  ability to get contraceptive coverage  because many will not be able to afford  to get it elsewhere  won't be able to find it elsewhere and  are going to have to do the kind of  search for contraceptives that they  should not be required to do and that  was not the intention of the affordable  care act that they had to do at kristen  wiig Iran agree with that were there any  surprises for you in this morning's  arguments well I think the biggest  surprise was just the extreme nature of  the counsel for Pennsylvania and New  Jersey in the sense that he at one  point even argued that only essentially  ministers should be entitled to an  exemption meaning that potentially even  the little sisters I mean we're talking  about nuns and when we want to talk  about reasonableness is it reasonable  that we would force Catholic nuns to  dole out birth control another petition  that's pending before the court has to  do with March for life which is a  pro-life organization that brings  millions to Washington DC each year they  would be required a pro-life  organization whose entire purpose is to  promote pro-life causes to potentially  use their money spend their money for  their employees to have a board of a  ssin coverage under their policies when  they can go and get that from other  places so this theoretical stuff of the  slippery slope when women can't find  birth control we're smart birth control  is easily accessible we can get it in  our doorstop overnight and the  government itself has admitted in this  case that there are many other means  that they can use to ensure women have  seamless coverage get their birth  control and their abortifacients without  forcing religious objectors to be  complicit in it and Kristen Wagner do  you think the courts going to take this  on head-on or just find a way to do a  technical ruling that will not take a  bigger issue in consideration oh you  know in our experience before the court  you never know exactly what it will do  and this is the sixth case in the last  eight years over this particular statute  so I think it's difficult to say I was  interested in justice Thomas's questions  related to standing meaning whether the  state's actually should be able to  present this claim and also this is a  little different than previous cases  involving what discretion does an agency  have in determining exemptions so I  think it could could be a little bit of  a boring decision in the sense of it  might focus on administrative discretion  and those kinds of things but it's  certainly important as you see how it  plays out and affects real people and  see vermigli you've sent through so many  court hearings over the years as a  lawyer and as a journalist was this any  different to see it this way with the  questions we certainly Justice Thomas  rarely asked her question he asked  plenty of questions today  I mean it's fascinating to get to hear  it live and not be in the courtroom  that's an unusual experience just as  Thomas I think probably felt that if the  Chief Justice was actually going to call  on him by name it was it was not a  situation in which you simply wanted to  say I pass and so he felt that he should  participate I wanted to just note one  thing which I think we heard most  clearly from Justice Breyer and that is  the courts frustration that this issue  hasn't been settled the court had this  case in 2016 it seemed to be deadlocked  at the time it is a ruling in which it  basically said let's send this back to  the lower courts though that the  government and the various parties and  the states and the courts can all figure  out a solution and Justice Breyer seemed  somewhat frustrated that here we are  again and still not clear what the  solution is that's right and Gloria all  we heard that from Pam you said why  can't we work this out is there a way to  balance these issues the legislating and  women's reproductive rights well I mean  I think that Justice Ginsburg was  essentially suggesting that you know  women also have to have their views  respected there should be tolerance for  their views you know not everybody  agrees with the anti-choice the an anti  contraceptive views of the so-called  pro-life religious groups there are many  people whose religions do not require  that that they give up contraceptives or  do not have access to contraceptives so  what about respect and tolerance for the  religious views of those who do not  agree for example with the views of the  Little Sisters of the Poor who are  Catholic and we try to respect  everyone's religious views in the United  States and not just the views of one  religion  right well this would be a closely  watched decision we've got about 20  seconds left and this already care to  predict the outcome no I can't predict  the outcome but I hope it's an outcome  and no one yeah all right well my thanks  to you Gloria Allred Kristin Wagoner  thank you so much appreciate you being  here  and professor Steve Vermeil from  American University Law School thank you  all for being here a fascinating morning  an hour and 40 minutes of contentious  debate back and forth  I'm Brian Ross thanks for 